runTransactionDbIO ::
  (IOE :> es) =>
  StockPolicy ->
  DBPool ->
  Eff (TransactionDb : es) a ->
  Eff es a
  ! signature: StockPolicy -> DBPool -> ...; the two callers are Server.Transaction.runTxEff and Server.SaleCommand.runSaleEff, both passing cfgStockPolicy (envConfig env)
  ! thin shim over DB.Transaction.*, DB.Transaction.Refund, DB.Transaction.Typed and DB.Reservation
  ! every write except CreateSale and UpdateSaleStatus is wrapped in try @InventoryException
  ! expectSaleTx / expectRefundTx call error when a row the DB layer just returned will not convert; that turns a conversion bug into a dead request (this is how the refund defect showed itself)
  > consumed by: Server.Transaction.runTxEff
  > consumed by: Server.SaleCommand.runSaleEff
  ? no test references

emptyTxStore :: TxStore
  ! stock policy is DoNotRestock for both void and refund. That is a test fixture, not a default of the running backend, which has none. A test of restocking sets tsStockPolicy itself.
  ? tested by: Test.Service.TransactionSpec.storeWith, Test.Service.TransactionSpec.storeWithItem, Test.Service.TransactionSpec.storeWithPayment, Test.Service.SaleSpec.emptySale, Test.Service.TransactionSpec.storeWithItemAndPayment, Test.Service.SaleSpec.saleWithItem, Test.Service.SaleSpec.saleWithItemAndPayment, Test.Service.TransactionSpec.spec, Test.Service.SaleSpec.spec

activeReservedQty :: UUID -> Map UUID ReservationEntry -> Int
  ! sums reservation.qty over entries with matching sku AND status == 'Reserved'
  ! filtering by status text (not by an enum) matches the DB row schema
  ? no test references

recomputeTotals :: Transaction -> Transaction
  ! mirror of DB.Transaction.updateTotalsS: subtotal, discount total and tax total from the lines, total = subtotal - discount + tax
  ? no test references

startProgress :: Transaction -> Transaction
  ? no test references

releaseReservedForTx :: UUID -> Map UUID ReservationEntry -> Map UUID ReservationEntry
  ? no test references

releaseOneReservation :: UUID -> UUID -> Int -> Map UUID ReservationEntry -> Map UUID ReservationEntry
  ? no test references

restockLines :: Transaction -> Map UUID Int -> Map UUID Int
  ! mirror of DB.Transaction.restockSaleS: adds each line's quantity to its sku in the stock map; a sku not in the map is skipped
  ! Covered by Test.Service.TransactionSpec since 2026-10-10: stock after void and after refund under each setting, a second void or refund adding nothing, and deleteSaleItem refusing a closed sale.
  ? no test references

finalizeProblemsFor :: Transaction -> [Text]
  ? no test references

runTransactionDbPure ::
  (GenUUID :> es, Clock :> es) =>
  TxStore ->
  Eff (TransactionDb : es) a ->
  Eff es (a, TxStore)
  ! intent: In-memory interpreter for unit tests; requires GenUUID and Clock
  ! TxStore holds maps for sales, item -> sale, payment -> sale, reservations and stock, plus tsStockPolicy
  ! hand-written mirror of the Postgres code: one line per sku, add carries an increment, totals recomputed by recomputeTotals, a Created sale moves to InProgress inside the add
  ! ENFORCES the same status rule as Postgres at each write and returns SaleNotOpen: add needs Created or InProgress; remove item, add payment and remove payment need InProgress; clear needs Created or InProgress; void needs Created, InProgress or Completed; refund needs Completed and not already refunded
  ! FinalizeSale uses Domain.SaleRules.finalizeProblems, the same function the Postgres code calls
  ! WriteRefund sets the original sale to Refunded and calls error if the refund's line or payment counts do not match the sale
  ! written by the same author in the same sittings as the Postgres code, so a misunderstood rule is likely wrong in both; nix/sale-checks is the independent evidence
  > consumed by: Test.Service.TransactionSpec
  > consumed by: Test.Service.SaleSpec
  ? tested by: Test.Service.TransactionSpec.runTest, Test.Service.SaleSpec.runTest, Test.Service.TransactionSpec.runTestWithEvents

%decision EffectfulOverMtlWithPureInterpreters
  what:    effectful with paired IO and pure interpreters per effect
  why:     effectful's Eff monad avoids the n-squared instances problem of mtl as effect counts grow. Every effect ships with both runXxxIO and runXxxPure. Service-layer code is unit-testable without a database, broadcaster, or wall clock. runClockPureSequence and runGenUUIDPure both accept a supply list and crash on exhaustion — explicit failure beats silent test nondeterminism. Cost: every DB action exists twice. Drift between IO and pure is a real risk; if it bites, fold the pure interpreter into a property test that compares the two.
  affects: runTransactionDbIO, runTransactionDbPure

%decision WritesReturnEitherInventoryException
  what:    AddSaleItem, DeleteSaleItem, AddSalePayment, DeleteSalePayment, FinalizeSale, ClearSale, VoidSale and WriteRefund return Either InventoryException; the effect carries the refusal as a value
  why:     The Postgres interpreter wraps each DB call in try @InventoryException. The in-memory interpreter applies the same status rule at the moment of the write and returns the same constructor. CreateSale and UpdateSaleStatus do not. UpdateSaleStatus writes a status with no lock and no check.
  affects: runTransactionDbIO, runTransactionDbPure, addSaleItem, deleteSaleItem, addSalePayment, deleteSalePayment, finalizeSale, clearSale, voidSale, writeRefund

%decision StockPolicyBelongsToTheInterpreter
  what:    What a void or refund of a completed sale does to stock is decided by the interpreter, not by the operation or the service
  why:     runTransactionDbIO takes a Domain.StockPolicy.StockPolicy as its first argument and passes restockOnVoid to DB.Transaction.voidTransaction and restockOnRefund to writeTypedRefund. runTransactionDbPure reads tsStockPolicy from its TxStore. Service.Transaction.voidTx and refundTx carry no policy, so their signatures and their tests did not change.
  affects: runTransactionDbIO, runTransactionDbPure, emptyTxStore, restockLines


